---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const policy = "Tideline privacy policy\n8 November 2026\n\nTideline is made by The Town Labs. It keeps a timeline of changes on your phone. It has no accounts, ads, analytics SDK or INTERNET permission. It does not send your timeline to the developer or operate a sync service.\n\nWhat Tideline reads\nTideline reads launcher-visible app names, package names, versions, install/update times, requested permissions and target Android versions. It records Android build/security-patch changes and battery level, charging state and reading times. Requested permissions do not mean an app was granted or used them.\n\nOptional Usage access\nOnly if you grant Usage access in Android settings, Tideline reads app-use durations and screen on/off and device restart events. It stores daily/hourly usage and screen-event coverage to show screen time and coverage-labelled overnight drain. Recording also runs periodically in the background; Android may defer it. You can use battery and change history without Usage access. Revoking access stops new usage collection and hides stored screen-time and overnight history; it does not erase those records.\n\nWhere records appear\nRecords are stored in the app's private timeline.db database. A home-screen widget you add gives its displayed summaries, app labels/icons and charts to your chosen Android launcher. Other people who can see your unlocked screen may see them. Opening Android app-info settings identifies the selected package to Android. Tideline has no timeline export or sharing action. Android, your launcher and any screenshots or device-level tools you choose are outside Tideline's network-permission boundary.\n\nStorage, retention and deletion\nTideline requests no cloud backup or device transfer of its app data and excludes its storage from Android backup/transfer rules. Device manufacturers and privileged device tools remain outside its control. The database is protected by Android's app sandbox, not a separate Tideline encryption password. History is retained until you clear Tideline's storage or uninstall it in Android settings. Both permanently remove local history; there is no recovery or developer-held copy. Revoking Usage access alone does not delete history. There is no account to delete.\n\nMeasurement limits\nAndroid decides how much usage history is available. Battery readings start when Tideline records them; missing readings stay missing. Overnight drain is a coarse, coverage-labelled screen-off trend, not a diagnosis. Nearby app or system changes do not establish what caused battery drain.\n\nContact and changes\nFor privacy questions, email abhibansal60@gmail.com. This policy describes this version's behavior. Future changes must be reflected here and in the store disclosures.\n";
const blocks = policy.trim().split('\n\n');
const [title, date] = blocks[0].split('\n');
---

<Base title="Tideline privacy policy" description="How Tideline by The Town Labs handles your local phone timeline.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated {date}.</p>
    {blocks.slice(1).map((block) => {
      const [heading, ...body] = block.split('\n');
      return body.length ? <section><h2>{heading}</h2><p>{body.join(' ')}</p></section> : <p>{heading}</p>;
    })}
  </article>
</Base>
